::U5L1Q01::A hazard is {=something with potential to cause harm ~the final medical diagnosis ~a guaranteed injury ~a type of treatment}

::U5L1Q02::Risk refers to {=likelihood and possible severity of harm ~only the existence of a hazard ~a medical license ~an emergency phone number}

::U5L1Q03::Which is a prevention strategy? {=Removing a loose cord from a walkway ~Waiting for an injury ~Ignoring warning signs ~Entering danger}

::U5L1Q04::Protective equipment is used to {=reduce injury risk ~eliminate every hazard ~replace supervision ~diagnose illness}

::U5L1Q05::A non-emergency situation has {=no immediate serious warning signs ~severe breathing difficulty ~unresponsiveness ~uncontrolled bleeding}

::U5L1Q06::Professional evaluation is appropriate when a concern is {=persistent, significant, uncertain, or beyond routine self-management ~always minor and resolved ~an immediate life threat only ~unrelated to health}

::U5L1Q07::Which is an emergency indicator? {=Severe breathing difficulty ~A stable small scrape ~Brief ordinary soreness ~A loose shoelace before activity}

::U5L1Q08::Scene safety means {=do not enter active danger ~rescue first regardless of danger ~move every injured person ~ignore environmental hazards}

::U5L1Q09::When immediate serious danger exists, a student should {=call emergency services now ~contact the TOR before 911 ~wait for symptoms to disappear ~investigate alone}

::U5L1Q10::A suspected poisoning should lead a student to {=contact appropriate emergency or poison help and follow directions ~give an unknown home remedy ~force vomiting ~wait several days}

::U5L1Q11::Why should a student avoid care beyond training? {=Untrained actions may increase harm and delay qualified help ~Training is never useful ~All injuries require no action ~Professionals cannot help}

::U5L1Q12::Unresponsiveness should be classified as {=an emergency warning sign ~a routine non-emergency ~proof of a diagnosis ~a reason to leave}

::U5L1Q13::Severe uncontrolled bleeding requires {=emergency help ~only later monitoring ~no response ~a social-media search}

::U5L1Q14::Which reduces distracted-driving risk? {=Keeping attention on driving and putting devices away ~Reading messages at stops ~Holding a phone lower ~Asking passengers to show videos}

::U5L1Q15::Heat illness risk can be reduced with {=hydration, breaks, shade, and appropriate supervision ~ignoring temperature ~heavy activity without rest ~avoiding fluids}

::U5L1Q16::Confusion and inability to stand during heat exposure indicate {=serious escalation requiring emergency response ~ordinary fatigue only ~a stable minor event ~proof of one specific diagnosis}

::U5L1Q17::A concussion is {=a brain injury requiring appropriate evaluation ~a minor skin scrape ~proof a person is fine ~prevented by ignoring symptoms}

::U5L1Q18::Which evidence supports professional evaluation for an ankle injury? {=Increasing pain and inability to bear weight ~Pain fully resolves ~Normal movement without discomfort ~No injury occurred}

::U5L1Q19::The safest response at a crash scene with leaking fuel is to {=stay out of danger and call emergency services ~enter immediately ~move through traffic ~use an open flame}

::U5L1Q20::A protective behavior is {=an action that reduces exposure or risk ~a diagnosis ~an unsafe rescue attempt ~a delayed emergency call}

::U5L1Q21::Why can a help level change? {=New or worsening evidence can indicate greater risk ~The first conclusion can never change ~All symptoms are identical ~Prevention replaces monitoring}

::U5L1Q22::Which statement uses evidence correctly? {=Because breathing is severely difficult, emergency help is needed ~The person definitely has one disease ~The situation feels strange so no help is needed ~All injuries are emergencies}

::U5L1Q23::An allergic reaction becomes an emergency when there is {=serious breathing difficulty or other immediate danger ~only a known allergy history ~no symptoms ~a request for water}

::U5L1Q24::First aid should be provided {=within a person’s training while appropriate help is obtained ~instead of emergency services ~without scene safety ~to diagnose the condition}

::U5L1Q25::Which order is safest? {=Check scene safety, recognize evidence, call appropriate help, follow trained directions ~Diagnose, enter danger, then wait ~Move everyone, then assess ~Ask social media first}

::U5L1Q26::Prevention is most effective when it occurs {=before harm and throughout an activity ~only after severe injury ~instead of supervision ~after all warning signs are ignored}

::U5L1Q27::Which situation is most likely urgent but not clearly an immediate emergency? {=Persistent significant pain requiring prompt evaluation ~Unresponsiveness ~Severe breathing difficulty ~Active severe bleeding}

::U5L1Q28::Why should a student cite scenario evidence? {=It supports the help-level conclusion ~It guarantees a diagnosis ~It replaces professional care ~It proves every possible cause}

::U5L1Q29::A bystander suggests unknown medicine for a worsening emergency. The student should {=reject the suggestion and obtain qualified emergency help ~follow it immediately ~delay the call ~hide the event}

::U5L1Q30::Knowing when not to manage an emergency alone protects safety by {=connecting serious danger with trained help promptly ~eliminating all responsibility ~requiring diagnosis ~making prevention unnecessary}
